type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

!! Overview

Adds four everyday fields to the tiddler edit template:

|!Field |!Row |!Control |!Stored value |
|`tools` |under Tags |list field, edited like tags |a list of titles |
|`project` |under Tags |single value, typed or picked |free text |
|`nature` |under Type |controlled vocabulary, single value |a bare slug (`concept`, `guide`, …) |
|`status` |under Type |controlled vocabulary, single value |a bare slug (`stable`, `raw`, …) |

The four controls come in two rows, each added to the edit template as an ordinary section: ''Tools'' and ''Project'' just under the tags row, ''Nature'' and ''Status'' just under the type row. No core tiddler is overridden and no core row is touched.

A row packs its blocks to the left at their natural width rather than spreading them across the tiddler, so that a block's own parts stay together — the delete button sits next to the control it clears rather than at the far end of a stretched column. Rows wrap when the tiddler gets too narrow.

The tools and tags pickers are strictly independent: the tools dropdown offers the values already used in `tools`, never the wiki's tags, and vice versa. Tool pills are rendered exactly like tag pills, per-value colour cascade included.

Each control carries the same clear button the core puts on Type, always visible. An empty Nature or Status shows a greyed hint in place of a value — a `<select>` has no placeholder attribute, so its blank first option plays that part. Hovering a label shows the field's real name.

The four fields are hidden from the list of fields at the bottom of the editor, through the core's own `$:/config/EditTemplateFields/Visibility/` mechanism: editing them twice on one form would be one place too many. Setting the corresponding tiddler to `show` brings any of them back.

!! Controlled vocabularies

A //slug// is the short, lowercase, unpunctuated identifier that stands for a value — `snippet`, `raw`. The word comes from the printing trade: a slug was a line of text cast in a single piece of metal, and newsrooms came to call the short working name written on a story in production its slug. Web publishing kept it for the identifier that stands for something in a URL, or here in a field.

Only the slug is ever written to the field. Icons and labels are display-only, resolved at render time, so a wiki stays readable and filterable in any language:

* the //allowed values and their order// live in the `list` field of `vocab/nature` and `vocab/status`
* the //icons// live in `vocab/icons.multids`, keyed `<field>/<value>`
* the //labels// live in `language/<lang>/vocab.multids`, keyed `Vocab/<field>/<value>`

A dropdown entry shows the label in the wiki's language, lowercased, followed by the stored value in brackets when the two differ — `extrait (snippet)` in French, plain `snippet` in English. Each value can also carry a one-line description, shown as a tooltip on hover: the `Vocab/<field>/<value>/Hint` strings, optional.

Adding, removing or reordering a value is done in the plugin source, not through the UI — there is no vocabulary editor, on purpose: a value added through the interface could not carry its translations. To add one, append its slug to the relevant `list` field, then add its icon and its label in each language. A value with no label falls back to its own slug; a value with no icon simply renders without one.

Shipped out of the box:

* `nature` — concept, ref, guide, pattern, snippet, template, decision, log, hub, task, tasklist, routine
* `status` — archived, deprecated, stable, raw

Helpers are exposed globally, for use in view templates and lists.

`bf-vocab-item` (icon + label) and `bf-vocab-option` (the dropdown form) are //procedures//. A procedure called as `<<name arg>>` receives its arguments as literal text, so a field's value has to be passed through a transclusion rather than a macro call:

```
<$transclude $variable="bf-vocab-item" field="nature" value={{!!nature}}/>
```

`bf-vocab-values`, `bf-vocab-label`, `bf-vocab-icon` and `bf-vocab-hint` are //functions//, and give the pieces separately. Call them through the `function` operator, which does bind their parameters:

```
<$let slug={{!!nature}}>{{{ [function[bf-vocab-label],[nature],<slug>] }}}</$let>
```

!! How it works

Everything is an edit-template section: four tiddlers tagged `$:/tags/EditTemplate`, placed with `list-after`, plus a stylesheet, some global procedures and the vocabularies. Nothing overrides a core tiddler, so a TiddlyWiki upgrade carries no risk of silently reverting part of the editor, and nothing collides with another plugin that touches the same rows.

Two details worth knowing:

# the tools and project inputs drive the core `keyboard-driven-input` macro directly rather than the `tag-picker` macro, which hardcodes the tags placeholder and reuses the tag input state the edit template defines once for the whole form — sharing it would let a half-typed tool name be added as a //tag// by the save keyboard shortcut
# their completion filters live on `search-filters`, read through `configTiddlerFilter` and `firstSearchFilterField`

!! Requirements

TiddlyWiki >= 5.3.0. No other dependency.

